From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VeilSilence <BoneFeaR@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 09:56:28 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] nvidia-drm: handle display events off the NVKMS event thread

The NVKMS event thread also delivers the flip completions that a
blocking atomic commit waits for, while holding connection_mutex. The
display change, content protection and HDCP topology handlers took
mode_config.mutex on that thread. The content protection work holds
mode_config.mutex and waits for connection_mutex, so a topology event
arriving during a blocking commit closed a cycle: the commit waited for
its flip event, which sat behind the topology handler, which waited for
the work, which waited for the commit. The commit then hit its 3 s
timeout, drained the flip list by hand and tripped the WARN in
__nv_drm_handle_flip_event() when the real event arrived.

The event thread now only records what changed per display under a
spinlock and queues the work. The work reads the HDCP topology through
a new NVKMS_IOCTL_GET_HDCP_TOPOLOGY with no DRM locks held, then
publishes content protection, topology and connection state under the
modeset locks. NVKMS no longer copies the topology into the connector
when sending the event; it only records whether it may be read. The
ioctl writes through a caller-supplied pointer, so it is refused for
user space clients.

The MST dynamic display connected event took mode_config.mutex on the
event thread too, in nv_drm_add_encoder(). It is recorded the same way;
the work adds the encoder with no DRM lock held, before publishing
anything else for that display, and sends the hotplug event.

The display event work now queues hotplug_event_work, so unload cancels
it before the hotplug work instead of after.

Co-Authored-By: Tom Englund <tomenglund26@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
